  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist. I haven't opened the dashboard to check how the panels render, and the rule hasn't been tested.\n\n**What the data holds:** Each document is one web request. The useful fields are `timestamp`, `response` (a string status code, queried through `response.keyword`), `bytes`, `extension`, `geo.src` and `geo.dest`, `clientip`, `machine.os`, `host` and `url`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-da765430 Web logs overview\" is at `http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-da765430-web-logs-overview-57c206073d36`. It defaults to the last 7 days and has five panels:\n- Total requests\n- Requests over time\n- Responses by status code\n- Average bytes by extension\n- Top source countries\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-da765430 5xx spike\" is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`.\n- **Check and window:** It runs every minute over a 5-minute window.\n- **Condition:** It fires when more than 10 documents match `response.keyword` of `5*`.\n- **State:** It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n- **Actions:** It has no action attached, so a firing shows up in Kibana but doesn't notify anyone.\n\nThe sample data is static, with timestamps from when it was loaded, so the rule may not fire unless new 5xx documents arrive.",
